# What one measurement of the workload benchmarks/normalize/workload names costs
# the normalize stage: one filer's facts, run into the history the stage writes.
# Recorded deliberately, read by crates/normalize/tests/bench.rs, and compared
# against the multiples in benchmarks/thresholds.
#
# allocations and bytes are counted, not timed, over the passes below, the
# output buffer growing to its size once and reused after that. Both numbers
# are the same on every machine.
allocations 283488
bytes 77204336

# How many times one measurement runs the stage over the workload: the count
# every number in this file was taken at, so changing it restates them all. One
# pass of the whole filer is about a tenth of a second in release, and the
# counted numbers are exact at any count, so one is enough to measure and few
# enough that the gate runner, which measures this once per proof, stays short.
passes 1

# The stage's time as a multiple of a plain copy of the workload's published
# bytes, measured in the same run — the machine's own speed is in both halves
# and divides out, which is what lets a number recorded on one machine mean
# anything on another. Far above one because the stage resolves every concept at
# every period, where the copy only moves the characters.
#
# Near the middle of what the machine that recorded it measured: sixteen runs,
# ranging from 12,902 to 13,382. A third significant figure here would be
# recording that noise, and it is the threshold beside it that decides anything,
# never this last digit.
cost 13000
